{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790888569,"nonce":"9hQ4FwVOLY9CJDnvXU9I72xWI+s=","title":"A quiet Incident cannot take back a re-firing Case, so a flapping alert draws a new Incident every time it fires","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0052 §4: a later matching Case joins within the Correlator's operator-written `quiet_grace` after the Incident went quiet; past it, it draws a new Incident. `61eeddf` joins only while active.\n\n**The demonstration.** A disk alert firing every 20 minutes with `quiet_grace` absent produces one Incident per firing overnight — the \"ten Incidents\" case the ADR names.\n\n**Why it matters.** Every Incident is declared outbound (ADR 0052 §5); ten Incidents is ten external incidents.\n\n**Where.** `quiet_grace_s` on the Correlator; the evaluator's join rule; the migration header must say why a grace window returns after `group_close_delay_s`/`refire_grace_s` left with `AlertGroup` (`00069`, `00071`; ADR 0039's \"the shape recurs\").\n\n**Scope.** small.\n\n**Done when.** With `quiet_grace_s=1800`, a re-fire 20 minutes after quiet joins the same Incident and it reads active again; a re-fire 40 minutes after opens a new Incident; tests pin both boundaries.\n\n**Blocked by.** `61eeddf`\n\n**Mode.** AFK — can be implemented and merged without a human decision. Source: ADR 0052/0053/0054 design session, 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}